  [38;5;153msearch          1.12.0    2/3     2026-09-29 10:10   failing: disk-space, index-freshness[39m
  You can see which services are failing and which checks, all in the table. You don't see the details ("timeout after
  5s"), and the row gets wide when several checks fail.

  [1mB. A HEALTH column plus a section underneath with the details (recommended)[0m
  [38;5;153mSERVICE         VERSION   READY   DEPLOYED           HEALTH[39m
  [38;5;153mapi-gateway     2.14.1    3/3     2026-09-29 14:02   ok[39m
  [38;5;153mbilling         4.2.3     2/3     2026-09-28 17:40   1/2 failing[39m
  [38;5;153msearch          1.12.0    2/3     2026-09-29 10:10   2/3 failing[39m

  [38;5;153mFailing checks:[39m
    [38;5;153mbilling         db-connection     timeout after 5s[39m
    [38;5;153mnotifications   queue-depth       12408 messages waiting
[39m    [38;5;153msearch          disk-space        91% used[39m
    [38;5;153msearch          index-freshness   last update 47 min ago[39m
  The table stays narrow and easy to scan, and the details tell you what to do next. It adds a few lines, and only when
  something is failing.

  [1mC. Column only ([38;5;153mok[39m / [38;5;153m1/2 failing[39m)[0m: the smallest change, but you'd still have to open the JSON to find out what's
  wrong.

  I recommend [1mB[0m. With either A or B I'd also add the count to the heading, e.g. [38;5;153mproduction: 10 services, 3 failing [39m
  [38;5;153mhealth checks, as of …[39m.

